# geo-ml: Mineral Prospectivity Mapping — WA

## TL;DR
ML-based mineral prospectivity mapping for Western Australia, targeting komatiite-hosted Ni-Cu sulfide (Eastern Goldfields) and LCT pegmatite lithium (Pilbara/SW WA). Uses publicly available geophysical/geochemical grids + OZMIN deposit locations as ground truth, trained with Positive-Unlabeled (PU) bagging to avoid false-negative contamination. Industry validation path through Adrian Herbert, Glencore Kalgoorlie (Mt Keith, Cosmos, Murrin Murrin nickel belt).

---

## Core Mechanisms

### Why ML Works Here
Mineral deposits are surface expressions of high-dimensional subsurface processes. Traditional prospectivity mapping uses expert-weighted evidence layers — effective but subjective, slow, and inconsistent across geologists. ML replaces the subjective weighting step: given known deposit locations and multi-layer geophysical signatures, learn the mapping `f(features) → P(deposit)`. The key leverage is that geophysical data is dense (every pixel has values) while deposits are sparse (hundreds of points across millions of pixels), making this a spatial anomaly detection problem.

### Nickel-Copper (Primary Target) — Geological Model
**Host:** 2.7 Ga komatiitic ultramafic sequences, Kalgoorlie and Kurnalpi Terranes, Eastern Goldfields, Yilgarn Craton.
**Mechanism:** High-degree partial melting of mantle produces Ni-rich komatiite magma. Magma is NOT sulfide-saturated at source — sulfides exsolve in situ through assimilation of crustal sulfur (sediments, felsic country rock) during emplacement. Sulfide liquid, being dense, pools in structural traps (embayments, fold hinges, channel-facies flows).
**Implication for features:** Need proxies for (1) komatiite presence (magnetic highs), (2) sulfur source (felsic crust = gravity lows, high Th radiometrics), (3) structural traps (magnetic derivatives, gravity gradients), (4) alteration (K radiometrics). WA hosts ~120 sulfide Ni + 20 lateritic Ni deposits — world's 3rd largest Ni sulfide province.

### Lithium (Secondary Target) — Geological Model
**Host:** LCT (lithium-cesium-tantalum) pegmatites, spodumene-bearing. Key regions: Pilbara Craton, SW Yilgarn (Greenbushes — world's largest hard-rock Li mine).
**ML finding:** Statistically verifiable proximity relationship between Li, Au, and Ni occurrences (Butterworth et al. 2025). Pegmatites preferentially occur along structural corridors near felsic intrusion margins.

