## Key Takeaways

1. The geological model is well-understood but has known blind spots — structural controls matter more than volcanic morphology, and geochemical indicators have higher false-positive rates than commonly assumed.
2. PU learning is the correct framework. Standard classification will produce misleadingly high metrics but geologically nonsensical maps.
3. Spatial validation is critical — random splits will flatter your model by 5–15 AUC points.
4. This is a district-scale corridor identification tool, not a drill-target selector. Set stakeholder expectations accordingly.
5. SHAP interpretability is the bridge between "interesting ML project" and "something a geologist will use."
6. Industry validation (Adrian Herbert / Glencore) should happen after the first prospectivity map is generated — show it to a domain expert and ask "does this make geological sense?"

